Definition 6.3 packages the separated sum of information systems A and B as the information system on sum tokens whose bottom is the sum bottom, whose consistent sets are those satisfying sum consistency, and whose entailment is sum entailment. A subset of a consistent set is consistent because the projections are monotone and an empty projection of the larger set forces the same projection of the subset to be empty, while the inhabited side remains consistent in A or in B. A singleton is consistent by the singleton computations: a left token has singleton left projection, consistent in A, and empty right projection; a right token is symmetric; the bottom has empty left projection, consistent in A, and empty right projection. Entailment of a token keeps the inserted set consistent, since inserting the bottom changes neither projection, inserting a left token adjoins it only on the left and the entailment has already forced the right projection to be empty, and inserting a right token is symmetric. Every consistent set entails the bottom. A token already present is entailed, because a left or right token makes its projection nonempty and therefore consistent, after which A or B reflects that member. Transitivity reduces the bottom to the trivial payload, and reduces a left or right token to transitivity in A or in B on the corresponding projections: the entailments of the intermediate tokens restrict to that summand, and the projection of the stronger set is nonempty because some member of the nonempty intermediate projection is entailed.
